  "query_preview": "..\n(Friday night through Wednesday)\nIssued at 345 AM CDT Thu Apr 23 2026\n\nThe extended forecast, as depicted by the LREF, will be a conveyor \nbelt of upper troughs, with a tertiary trough making landfall on the \nwest coast near the Bay Area Saturday evening, followed by a fourth \ntrough along the same path making landfall Tuesday evening.  The net \neffect of this will be warm and dry conditions for West Texas and \nSoutheast New Mexico, with strong westerly flow aloft resulting in \npossible high winds off and on through the Guadalupes/Delawares \nthrough at least next Thursday morning.  As noted above, high winds \nin the mountains will diminish this evening, but return Saturday \nnight as the tertiary trough approaches.  Attm, winds look to peak \nSunday night with the passage of the trough through West Texas and \nSoutheast new Mexico.  High winds then redevelop beginning Tuesday \nnight w/the approach of the fourth trough.\n\nThese high winds through next week will result in warm and mostly \ndry conditions, keeping highs well above normal through at least \nSunday, when highs should peak for this forecast at over 10 F above \nnormal on average.  A Pac front is forecast to drop in behind the \ntertiary trough on Sunday night, dropping highs Monday through \nWednesday down to ~ 5-10 F above normal.  Beginning Tuesday evening, \nsome of the long range models hint at convection developing in the \neast along/east of a dryline, but this activity looks isolated at \nbest that far out.",
